---
name: sudo-elevation
description: Use when a task needs root via sudo on this machine, when running commands as root, or when sudo reports that a password is required. Covers non-interactive `sudo -n`, time-boxed lease requests via `sudo-elevation request`, and the status/lock commands.
---

# sudo-elevation (v@@VERSION@@, base @@BASE_HUMAN_EN@@)

Rule: always try `sudo -n <cmd>` first. Never retry bare `sudo` on a password prompt; request a lease instead.

## Commands

```bash
sudo -n <cmd>                                            # normal path
sudo-elevation request --for <DUR> --reason "<task>"     # GUI approval, user may change DUR
sudo-elevation status [--porcelain]                      # remaining lease; --porcelain is KV for scripts
sudo-elevation lock                                      # revoke cache and restore base window
sudo-elevation parse <DUR>                               # debug duration spec
```

DUR: `90s` / `45m` / `2h` / `1d`, bare number = minutes, `until-lock` = infinite until lock.
Dialog times out in ~5min; expiry mid-task kills later `sudo -n`, so re-request.

## Duration

You estimate `--for`; user overrides in dialog.

- Attended (user waiting): short, e.g. `15m`; re-approve anytime.
- Unattended: max needed, e.g. `2h`~`12h`; no second chance if it expires.
- Typical: single install/restart `15m`; long download/build `2h`~`12h`. Avoid `until-lock` unless user agrees; it must be locked after use (verify `status` shows no lease).

## No GUI

Ask the human to run in their terminal (never run `grant` yourself):

- `sudo -v` (base window @@BASE_HUMAN_EN@@ only)
- `sudo-elevation grant --for 1h --reason "..."` (any duration)

## Never

- No `sudo -S`, `echo <pw> | sudo`, `expect`, or stored passwords.
- No `/etc/sudoers*` edits or bypassing sudo-elevation.
- Never ask for passwords; passwords go to system dialogs only.
- Window, not command: any same-user process can sudo inside. Keep `--reason` short and true (user sees it; spoofable).
